  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Tongues of Fire is a rust fungus in the Gymnosporangiaceae family that alternates between juniper and apple/hawthorn hosts throughout its life cycle. In New England, it is recognized by its distinctive bright orange, gelatinous fruiting bodies that emerge from infected juniper branches, particularly in spring.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Orange Gelatinous Horns",
      "description": "Bright orange to reddish, horn-like or tongue-shaped gelatinous structures (telial horns) that emerge from juniper bark, typically 1-3 inches long",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Host Plant",
      "description": "Found on eastern red cedar and other Juniperus species in New England; the fungus requires juniper as its primary host",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Seasonal Appearance",
      "description": "Most visible in spring (April-May) when gelatinous structures are swollen with moisture; spores spread to alternate apple and hawthorn hosts",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Tongues of Fire occurs in mixed woodlands and ornamental landscapes throughout New England where eastern red cedar and apple or hawthorn trees grow in proximity.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "This fungus has a complex two-host life cycle, spending part of its life on juniper where it produces the showy orange horns, then alternating to apple and hawthorn trees where it causes a different symptom called cedar-apple rust.",
